-- The orphan rule, on a head whose local type is behind a structural one - doc/spec/classes.md, and
-- Analysis-Status.md §6's type.def.
--
-- `Eq` is Core's and `Local` is this module's, so both instances below are placed by the type: the
-- module that declared `Local` is the one that may say what equality means for a thing made out of
-- it. Reject.Orphan.yana is the other side of the same rule.
--
-- The reason this is a fixture is that the walk answering "does this head mention a type of mine"
-- had arms for a record, a tuple, a pointer, a borrow and a fixed array, and none for a function, a
-- vector or an atomic - so a head that reached its local type through one of the last three found
-- nothing and was reported as an orphan, with advice to move the instance into the module it was
-- already in. Both heads here were refused before the `kTypeChildren` column existed to check that
-- walk against.

import Native

data Local = Local

fn measure(x: Local) -> Int = 3

-- Through a function type, which is what `Fun`'s missing arm cost.
instance Eq((Local) -> Int):
    fn ==(lhs: (Local) -> Int, rhs: (Local) -> Int) -> Bool = True
    fn !=(lhs: (Local) -> Int, rhs: (Local) -> Int) -> Bool = False

-- And through an atomic pointer, which is what `Atomic`'s cost. `Atomic(a)` admits an integer, a
-- `Bool` or a pointer (Analysis-Atomics.md §3.2), so a local type reaches one only as `%Local` -
-- which is a raw pointer holding a declared type, and is exactly the shape the walk descends.
instance Eq(Atomic(%Local)):
    fn ==(lhs: Atomic(%Local), rhs: Atomic(%Local)) -> Bool = True
    fn !=(lhs: Atomic(%Local), rhs: Atomic(%Local)) -> Bool = False

fn main() -> Int = if measure == measure then measure(Local) else 0
